.TH SUM 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
sum \- print a 16-bit checksum and block count of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sum
.RB [ \-rs ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sum
prints a 16-bit checksum and the size in blocks of each
.IR file ,
by one of two traditional algorithms.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
The options choose the algorithm and the block size.
Bytes are unsigned in both.
.TP
BSD
Used by default and with
.BR \-r .
The checksum starts at zero.
For each byte it is rotated right by one bit within 16 bits, the byte is
added, and the result is cut to 16 bits.
The size is in blocks of 1024 bytes.
.TP
System V
Used with
.BR \-s .
The checksum is the sum of all the bytes, kept to 32 bits.
It is reduced to 16 bits by adding its high half to its low half, and
then again to absorb the carry.
The size is in blocks of 512 bytes.
.PP
A size is rounded up to a whole block, so empty input has size 0.
.PP
Each BSD line is the checksum as 5 digits with leading zeros, a space,
the size right-aligned in at least 5 columns, a space, and the file name
as given
on the command line.
The name is printed only when there is more than one operand.
With one operand, or none, nothing follows the last space.
.PP
Each System V line is the checksum, a space, the size, a space, and the
name.
The name is always printed, as
.B \-
when standard input is read because there are no operands.
.PP
A file that cannot be opened or read is reported on standard error and
has no output line.
The remaining files are still processed.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-rs ,
and may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options besides
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B \-r
Use the BSD algorithm and 1024-byte blocks.
This is the default.
It wins over
.B \-s
wherever the two appear, so
.B \-sr
and
.B \-rs
both select it.
.TP
.B \-s
Use the System V algorithm and 512-byte blocks.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows shows its critical-error dialog for an operand on a drive with
no disk in it.
Otherwise the file fails to open.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded as the program starts, as needed
when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, and the console interprets the
bytes of file names in its own code page.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every file was summed, or
.B \-\-help
was given.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be opened or read, or the command line was invalid.
An invalid option also prints a usage message on standard error.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader exited before all output was
written.
If the pipe breaks only when the output still buffered at exit is
written, or standard output fails in any other way, the status is not
affected.
.PP
A file that cannot be opened is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sum: can\(aqt open \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
and an error while reading as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sum: \fIfile\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Checksum the output of a command with the System V algorithm:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf abc | sum \-s
294 1 \-
.EE
.RE
.PP
Checksum two files, which makes the BSD form print their names:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sum a.txt b.txt
16556     1 a.txt
36979     1 b.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print only the checksum of one file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sum a.txt | cut \-d\(aq \(aq \-f1
16556
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Both checksums are weak.
They miss many changes and do not resist tampering.
Use
.BR sha256sum (1)
or another digest where it matters.
.PP
.B \-s
is GNU
.BR sum 's
.BR \-\-sysv .
.PP
Files and standard input are read as binary, so the checksum covers the
exact bytes.
A text file with CR LF line endings has a different checksum from a copy
with LF line endings.
Output lines end with LF.
.PP
Standard input is not closed after it is read.
A second
.B \-
operand reads what remains, which at end of file is nothing, so its
checksum and size are 0.
.PP
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.PP
.I file
may be a Windows path, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or
a busybox-w32 device name such as
.IR /dev/null .
It is printed exactly as given.
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
.PP
Wildcards are expanded by the C runtime when busybox starts, even in
quoted operands; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is, and fails with
.BR "Invalid argument" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cksum (1),
.BR md5sum (1),
.BR sha256sum (1)
